<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kannauj</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanauj, Kanyakubja, Mahodaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kannauj is an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> as Kanyakubja. From the seventh century it was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> and then the prize contested by the Pratiharas, Palas and Rashtrakutas, and it served as the seat of the Gurjara-Pratihara and later Gahadavala kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> plundered it in 1018. It is famous for its perfumes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kannauj.jpg" alt="Kannauj" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nature lusty vikash · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goddess_Annapurna_Temple_,tirwa_town,stateUttar_Pradesh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The greatest victory came under Pulakeshin II, who halted the southward advance of Harsha, ruler of <b>Kannauj</b>, on the line of the Narmada River.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He took the Ismaili-ruled city of Multan in 1006 and again in 1010, defeated Anandapala at Chach in 1008, plundered Nagarkot, Thanesar, Mathura and <b>Kanauj</b>, and in 1025–1026 marched across the Thar desert to sack the temple of Somnath on the Gujarat coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Nagabhata II took <b>Kannauj</b> from the Ayudha dynasty around 816 and made it the imperial capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He extended Pala rule over Bihar, pushed into the Ganges valley and, after setbacks against the Gurjara-Pratihara king Vatsaraja and the Rashtrakuta Dhruva, installed his own candidate, Chakrayudha, on the throne of <b>Kannauj</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dynasty’s wars were dominated by the so-called tripartite struggle with the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty and the Pala Empire of Bengal for control of <b>Kannauj</b> and the Ganges plain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pulakeshin II, king of the Chalukyas of Badami, issued the Bijapur-Mumbai grant inscription, which mentions his victory over Harsha of <b>Kanauj</b> on the Narmada.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-4">4 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
